Say what the second receive returned, because its only report goes to a log with no sink.

A listener gets its first message and then goes deaf while every layer below reports success. The
daemon delivers a read reply carrying an event, libkqueue delivers it to its caller, and libxpc is
never called. This is the step in between, and it turns out not to be a delivery at all.

On a MACH_RCV_TOO_LARGE event, libdispatch does not receive anything from the kevent. It reads the
PORT NAME out of ke->data and goes and receives the message ITSELF, with a mach_msg call carrying
MACH_RCV_TIMEOUT. When that fails, the only trace of it is _dispatch_bug_mach_client at the out
label, which goes through _dispatch_log to syslog, and this container has no syslog sink. Silent by
construction.

MEASURED, the whole chain in one run, with the daemon and libkqueue and libxpc traces interleaved:

    copyout 0x803  TOO_LARGE -> drain port=0x803  size=120 kr=0x0        -> libxpc received  poke 1
    copyout 0x1503 TOO_LARGE -> drain port=0x1503 size=140 kr=0x0        -> libxpc received  peer
    copyout 0x803  no event available, dropping
    copyout 0x803  TOO_LARGE -> drain port=0x803  size=120 kr=0x10004008 -> FAILED, dropped   poke 2

0x10004008 is MACH_RCV_INVALID_DATA. So THE GUEST OWN mach_msg RECEIVE FAILS, on the second receive
from the same port, with the same size and the same code path as the first. Nothing above libdispatch
is at fault and neither is libdispatch: it is asking the kernel for a message and being refused.

That moves the question into our mach_msg receive path, which is where it should have been two
corrections ago.
--- a/src/event/event_kevent.c
+++ b/src/event/event_kevent.c
@@ -19,6 +19,8 @@
  */
 
 #include "internal.h"
+#include <stdio.h>
+#include <stdlib.h>
 #if DISPATCH_EVENT_BACKEND_KEVENT
 #if HAVE_MACH
 #include "protocol.h"
@@ -3167,6 +3169,22 @@
 	hdr = malloc(siz); // mach_msg will return TOO_LARGE if hdr/siz is NULL/0
 	kr = mach_msg(hdr, options, 0, dispatch_assume(hdr) ? siz : 0,
 			(mach_port_name_t)ke->data, MACH_MSG_TIMEOUT_NONE, MACH_PORT_NULL);
+	/*
+	 * WHAT THIS SECOND RECEIVE ACTUALLY RETURNED.
+	 *
+	 * A listener gets its first message and then goes deaf while every layer below reports success:
+	 * the daemon delivers a read reply carrying an event, libkqueue delivers it to its caller, and
+	 * libxpc is never called. This is the step in between, and it is not a delivery at all -- on a
+	 * MACH_RCV_TOO_LARGE event libdispatch goes and receives the message ITSELF from ke->data, with
+	 * MACH_RCV_TIMEOUT and a zero timeout, so a message that is not instantly available is simply
+	 * lost. When it fails, the only report is _dispatch_bug_mach_client at `out`, which goes to a
+	 * log with no sink in this container: silent by construction.
+	 */
+	if (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_DISPATCH") != NULL && getenv("CIDER_TRACE_DISPATCH")[0] != '\0') {
+		fprintf(stderr, "CIDER_DISPATCH machport drain: port=0x%x size=%u second receive kr=0x%x\n",
+				(unsigned)(mach_port_name_t)ke->data, (unsigned) siz, (unsigned) kr);
+		fflush(stderr);
+	}
 	if (likely(!kr)) {
 		flags |= DISPATCH_EV_MSG_NEEDS_FREE;
 		return _dispatch_kevent_mach_msg_recv(du, flags, hdr, msg_pp, ovr_pp);
@@ -3183,6 +3201,11 @@
 
 out:
 	if (unlikely(kr)) {
+		if (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_DISPATCH") != NULL && getenv("CIDER_TRACE_DISPATCH")[0] != '\0') {
+			fprintf(stderr, "CIDER_DISPATCH machport drain FAILED, kr=0x%x, the message is dropped\n",
+					(unsigned) kr);
+			fflush(stderr);
+		}
 		_dispatch_bug_mach_client("_dispatch_kevent_mach_msg_drain: "
 				"message reception failed", kr);
 	}
